# Pre-selection instructions

Insert a PRE-SELECTION stage between discovery and evaluation:

```
DISCOVERY > PRE-SELECTION > EVALUATION > SORTING & SELECTION
   L               N              N              M
```

It cuts L discovered job ads to the N that get fully evaluated, using only data already
available at discovery time.

Today the cut is `new_job_ads[:limit]` in `discovery.discover_jobs`, over a list ordered
by insertion into the aggregation dict: query order, then JobSpy order within a query. With
`--limit 10` and 10 results per query the run evaluates the first query's results and
nothing else.

Pre-selection targets selecting ads which have the highest chance to lead to a high evaluation, with limited computational cost.

## Invariants

1. Pre-selection favours programmatic checks which do not require LLM calls. These may include de-duplicate, & regexpr.
2. Pre-selection can include up to a fixed number of LLM calls (1 - 3) per discovery run. Number of calls must not grow with L.
3. Every dropped job ad is reported with the stage that dropped it.
4. `--no-preselect` restores today's behaviour, so the two are comparable on one job ad
   set.
5. No candidate content in `.py`. Role, domain and location knowledge comes from
   `resume.md` / `job_preferences.md` / the rubric.

L is bounded at roughly 100 (4-10 drafted queries x `--max-results`, default 10), and a
~150-token job ad summary puts the full set around 15k tokens against Haiku 4.5's 200k
input window. Assert the job ad count and warn as it approaches the budget: if L outgrows
it, a ranked cut becomes necessary and invariant 2 has to be revisited.



# Current implementation plan:

## Prerequisite: retain the discovery data already being fetched

`jobspy.scrape_jobs` returns 34 columns; `discovery.jobspy_search` keeps four (`url`,
`title`, `company`, `location`). Retain also: `description`, `is_remote`, `date_posted`,
`job_type`, `job_level`, `min_amount` / `max_amount` / `currency`, `company_industry`,
`work_from_home_type`.

Set `linkedin_fetch_description=True`. Indeed populates `description` already; LinkedIn
needs the flag, at one extra HTTP request per result (latency and block risk, no API spend).
Without it, LinkedIn job ads are judged on titles while Indeed ones are judged on ad
text. Measure the added latency on the first run.


## Stage 1: deterministic pass (free, no LLM, any L)

Drops and annotations only, no ordering. Run the steps in this order.

**1. Drop Stale Job Ads.** Drop job ads whose `date_posted` is older than `MAX_AGE_DAYS`
(45). A missing `date_posted` is kept: absent data is not evidence. Staleness is the only unconditional drop. The cutoff should eventually become a user parameter rather than a constant.

Location and work mode play no part in pre-selection. Remoteness reaches the score later through ranking's commute modifier.


**2. Duplicate collapse.** One job opening at one company appears once.

`filter_new_job_ads` dedupes on normalized URL only, so the same posting on LinkedIn, Indeed and the company's careers page survives as three job ads. Add a second key,
`job_opening_key()`: normalized `(company, title)`, lowercased and whitespace-collapsed, with gender markers
(`(m/f/d)`, `(h/f/x)`) stripped and `Sr.`/`Snr`/`Jr` expanded. Location suffixes stay: the same role advertised in two cities is two job openings with different commutes.

Applied in two places, both owned by `preselection.py` so the key has one owner
(`filter_new_job_ads` keeps only the URL check):

- within the run, collapsing duplicates to one job ad;
- against storage, dropping job ads whose `(company, title)` was already evaluated. Needs
  a new read-only accessor in `storage.py` returning `(normalized_url, company, job_title,
  application_status)`; `list_evaluated_urls()` returns URLs only. No schema change.

`application_status` is ignored. Having been evaluated is the drop rule on its own, and a
discarded role is not evidence about a different role at the same company, so a discard
never suppresses the employer.

The survivor of a collapse is the URL most likely to scrape into a full ad: prefer a domain
absent from `_AGGREGATOR_DOMAINS` (the company's own careers page), then a source board,
then a third-party re-poster. Attach the discarded siblings to the survivor as `alternates`.
When `evaluate_job` raises `ScrapeError`, discovery tries those alternates before calling
`find_company_posting_url`, which otherwise pays a Tavily search plus an LLM call to
rediscover a URL this stage already had.

**3. Rubric prescore**: `evaluate_rubric(rubric, match_text(title, location, description))`,
over the **full** description since regex has no token budget. Attach the signed score and
matched criterion names to each job ad as grounding for stage 2, the role the rubric
already plays in `compatibility_score`. It is an annotation, never a filter: an unmatched
criterion on thin ad text is evidence of thin text, not of a bad job.

## Stage 2: LLM batch judgment (one call)

One call, with invariant 2's remaining budget left unspent: a screen pass is added only if
evaluation shows one call is not enough.

Input: every surviving job ad as a summary with a short integer id, plus the resume and
the preferences. Output: the N selected ids with a one-line reason each. N defaults to 10.

The excerpt is anchored, not head-truncated: the opening of an ad is company blurb and the
close is benefits boilerplate, so the window starts at the first responsibilities /
requirements anchor, ends at an offer / benefits anchor occurring past 60% of the text, and
caps at `EXCERPT_CHARS` (900). No anchor match falls back to head truncation.

Measured over `evals/ads/`: a requirements anchor appears in 7/7 ads at 21-79% of the way
in, a responsibilities anchor in 6/7 at 11-43%, an offer anchor in 4/7 with three past 76%
and one false positive at 8% inside a company blurb, which is what the 60% guard exists for.
Headings appear in 0/7 and bullets in 4/7, so neither can anchor the window. Those fixtures
are `scrape.py` output; JobSpy descriptions are markdown and likely more structured, so the
heuristic needs re-measuring against real discovery output.

Validate as `_validate_queries` does: drop out-of-range ids with a warning, collapse
duplicates, backfill a short reply by prescore order so the stage always returns exactly N.

Do not chunk. Chunking makes calls O(L) and breaks invariant 2.

Present job ads with explicit ids and prescore annotations rather than as a bare ordered
list, to blunt position bias over a ~100-item list. If evaluation shows position bias
anyway, fix it with a deterministic interleave of presentation order, not a ranked cut.

Query-coverage diversity is not enforced: with no truncation, every query's results compete
on merit, and a query contributing nothing to the selected N is a signal about the query.
Per-company caps and role-family spread are deferred until a run shows one of them binding.

## Interfaces

- New module `jobsearch/preselection.py` (mocked in place) exporting
  `preselect(job_ads, n, rubric, resume, preferences, known_job_openings)`, returning
  `{"selected", "dropped", "stats"}` with every discovered job ad appearing exactly once
  across the first two. `discovery.py` calls it; it does not call back into discovery, opens
  no database and reads no files, so stage 1 is unit-testable offline.
- `storage.py` gains `list_evaluated_job_openings() -> [(normalized_url, company, job_title,
  application_status)]`, supplying `known_job_openings`.
- `discover_jobs` calls `preselect()` after `filter_new_job_ads()` (which keeps its
  URL-level check), prints `format_preselection()` in place of `_print_job_ads()`, and
  evaluates `result["selected"]`. Its `except ScrapeError` block tries the survivor's
  `alternates` before `find_company_posting_url()`. `evaluation.py` is untouched.
- `discover_jobs` resolves the rubric once, before discovery, and passes it to `preselect()`.
  `evaluate_job()` loads the rubric per job, so an unpinned recompile partway through a run
  would prescore against one rubric and score against another.
- `discover_jobs.py`: `--no-preselect`, and a flag to disable the LinkedIn description fetch.
- `--limit` becomes N, the pre-selection target, not a truncation of the tail, defaulting to
  `DEFAULT_N` (10) instead of today's "evaluate everything".
- Without `--evaluate`, pre-selection still runs and `_print_job_ads` lists every
  job ad, marking the selected N and the stage that dropped each of the others
  (invariant 3). This makes a bare listing cost one LLM call where it previously cost none;
  `--no-preselect` is the zero-call path.

## Evaluation

Pre-selection is a selector, so pass/fail on one run says nothing. Measure precision@N: of
the job ads kept, what fraction score above a threshold once fully evaluated. Recall needs
one batch where all L were evaluated, affordable at small L, and is the only way to detect a
good job being dropped.

Add a fixture holding a captured job ad list as returned by discovery, under `evals/`.
The existing `evals/ads/` files are single postings and cannot exercise a stage whose input
is a list. That fixture is data: it never reaches a `.py` file, and nothing under `evals/`
touches `data/evaluations.db`.

Unit-test stage 1 offline: the duplicate key, survivor choice and alternates, the staleness
drop, the prescore annotation, id validation against a fabricated reply, and the backfill
path.

## Open

No decisions remain open. Two things need measuring against real discovery output rather
than deciding:

1. The section anchors, calibrated on `scrape.py` fixtures, against JobSpy's markdown
   descriptions.
2. Whether the dedup key's noise stripping catches the variants boards actually produce.

## Deferred

The end-to-end "propose M jobs" entrypoint. `discover_jobs.py --evaluate` stops at
evaluation, and M (jobs proposed) would need to be distinguished from N (jobs evaluated) at
the CLI. Ranking, which it would also compose, lives on branch `feat/job-ranking`.
